{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BoabuZTHNCr3Fnkvn5XqdF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the research project deeply to define three tiers (best/median/worst) for the Dream simulation. I need to consider:\n\n1. The current state of experiments - what's done, what's running, what's planned\n2. The paper framing and claims\n3. The key uncertainties that remain\n4. The venue target (EMNLP 2026 Main)\n\nKey uncertainties/pending items:\n- plan_014 (14B scale validation, Qwen2.5-14B) is RUNNING - this is critical for the \"scale robustness\" claim\n\n\n- plan_023 (theory section) is planned but not done\n- plan_015 (wild-data calibration) is planned\n- plan_018 (Binoculars/Fast-DetectGPT baseline) is planned\n- plan_020 (bootstrap CIs) is planned\n- DCOL v6 failed (negative) - already handled by contingency\n- Instruct model (plan_021) showed weak signal (47.4% acc) - a limitation\n- Prompted generation showed signal persists but weaker (69.5% vs 83.3%)\n- Cross-strategy pipeline works but strategy classifier is weak (57.8%)\n\nNow I'm thinking through what defines each tier by looking at the critical assumptions and how robust the core claims are under different scenarios. to evaluate:**\n1. 14B scale validation succeeds (plan_014 RUNNING)\n2. Theory section provides adequate theoretical grounding\n3. The \"no algorithmic contribution\" concern is adequately addressed\n4. Cross-strategy pipeline is compelling enough as a practical contribution\n5. Reviewer perception of \"empirical study\" paper type\n6. Feature universality holds at 14B scale\n7. Instruct model weakness doesn't undermine generality claims\n\n**Best case assumptions:**\n- 14B experiment succeeds with strong signal (comparable to 1B results)\n- Theory section connects convincingly to model collapse literature\n- Reviewers value task formalization + Atlas as primary contributions\n- Cross-strategy pipeline + Atlas + EDD seen as sufficient contributions\n- EMNLP \"New Missions\" track is a good fit\n- The 3-model × 3-strategy coverage is seen as thorough\n\n**Median case:**\n- 14B experiment shows degraded signal but still meaningful results\n- Theory section is adequate though not particularly compelling\n- Reviewers acknowledge novelty but want more than pure empirical work\n- Some of my claims will need to be softened\n\n**Worst case:**\n- 14B experiment fails or produces contradictory results\n- Theory section doesn't adequately address the \"no contribution\" concern\n- Reviewers dismiss it as purely empirical without methodological innovation\n- Significant reframing would be necessary\n\nNow I'm defining concrete metrics to track across these scenarios. I need to monitor the 14B 4-class accuracy results, check whether feature universality holds at that scale, assess how well the theory section lands, and track the cross-strategy pipeline improvements alongside EDD consistency.\n\nLooking at the existing performance ranges across smaller models, I see 4-class accuracy spans from 69.6% up to 85.4%, with d1v2 AUC ranging from 0.751 to 0.937, and the cross-strategy pipeline delivering an 11.5 percentage point improvement. EDD consistently equals 4 across the three models tested. For the 14B experiment, I'm expecting either strong results above 75% accuracy with feature universality intact and EDD=4, or a median scenario where accuracy falls between 60-75% with partial feature alignment and possibly EDD=3.\n\nNow I'm thinking through how these experimental outcomes map to paper reception. The best case would be an accept at EMNLP Main with a 4.0 score, the median would land in borderline territory around 3.0-3.5 requiring revisions, and the worst case would be a 2.5-3.0 rejection that's still reframeable for resubmission. But I'm realizing I should be defining the three tiers based on core assumptions about what could realistically happen with the remaining experiments and how that shapes reviewer perception, rather than just predicting outcomes. The key uncertainty I need to account for is the 14B scale validation that's currently running. biggest remaining risk. The theory section quality is crucial—can it really address the \"no algorithmic contribution\" concern? I also need to verify whether existing detectors like Binoculars and FastDetectGPT actually fail on the ordinal task, which would strengthen our novelty claim. And there's the question of whether reviewers will accept an empirical study plus task formalization as sufficient contribution.\n\nLet me map out the outcome tiers. In the best case, the 14B experiments confirm our findings with accuracy above 72%, the features generalize across models, and the theory section cleanly connects model collapse mechanisms to our detection approach—while existing detectors fail at ordinal detection. That puts us at a 3.5-4.0 reviewer score and acceptance. The median scenario has partial confirmation with 14B accuracy between 58-72%, features that show up in the top features but maybe not dominantly, and a theory section that's descriptive rather than predictive. Some detectors might work weakly. That's borderline territory around 3.0-3.5. The worst case is 14B accuracy below 58% or completely different top features, unconvincing theory, and existing detectors matching our performance—that's rejection territory but potentially reframeable.\n\nNow I need to think through what the 14B results might actually reveal and how they interact with my other assumptions, especially considering the instruct model only achieved 47.4% accuracy. and what that means.\n\nThe instruct model result is already known - it's weak. This is a limitation that will be present in all three tiers. The question is how much weight reviewers give it.\n\nLet me define tiers more carefully:\n\n**BEST (all key assumptions hold)**:\n- A1: 14B scale validation succeeds (4-class acc ≥ 72%, p90_ppl/var_surprisal in top-3)\n- A2: Theory section provides testable predictions that match experimental data\n- A3: Existing detectors (Binoculars, Fast-DetectGPT) cannot do ordinal depth estimation (<40% 4-class)\n- A4: Instruct model weakness is accepted as expected (RLHF flattens depth signal) with brief analysis\n- A5: 3/3 model seeds confirm stability (already 2/3 done)\n\nLooking at the actual results: the 14B model hits the accuracy targets and shows strong AUC across both distance metrics, with p90_ppl and var_surprisal emerging as the dominant features. Existing detectors fall well short of ordinal classification, and the theory section successfully derives an upper bound on effective depth that aligns with what we observe empirically. The complete pipeline—from task formalization through the depth degradation atlas to the cross-strategy approach—forms a cohesive contribution that connects to the broader model collapse literature, putting this solidly in accept territory.\n\nThe median scenario shows degradation but not collapse. The 14B signal weakens (55-72% accuracy, 0.65-0.80 AUC), and var_surprisal drops out of the top features while p90_ppl remains. The theory section becomes more of a post-hoc explanation than a predictive framework. Existing detectors still can't do ordinal classification, but the gap narrows. This is still publishable but with caveats about scope and generalization.\n\nFor the narrative, I need to reframe this as scale-dependent attenuation rather than universal robustness. The 1-1.5B results hold up, but 14B shows real degradation that suggests the signal isn't as fundamental as initially claimed. The theory section shifts from explanatory to motivational. The cross-strategy pipeline is still a contribution, though EDD might be 3 instead of 4 at larger scales. I should acknowledge that existing detectors are catching some of what we're finding at shallow depths.\n\nThe worst case is if 14B completely fails—either accuracy drops below 55%, features shift entirely, or our shallow-depth performance matches existing detectors. That would undermine the core claim about depth as a universal signal. The theory section would also need to explain why the signal doesn't scale, which it currently can't.\n\nLooking at the metrics, 14B accuracy ranges from 35-55%, which is concerning. The AUC is weak, EDD drops to 2 or less, and the top features don't overlap with 1B at all. Existing detectors are hitting 50-65% accuracy, so we're not clearly ahead. The theory can't derive consistent bounds. This pushes the paper toward rejection territory, though there's a path forward by reframing entirely—moving from \"universal depth estimation framework\" to \"scale-dependent depth signal characterization.\" The real contribution becomes documenting that depth signals are strong at smaller scales and then degrade, which is still interesting but more limited in scope.\n\nNow I'm thinking about what \"worst\" actually means for the theory section. If the theory can't be written convincingly, that's a separate problem from the 14B failure. I should be thinking about how these failure modes correlate—if 14B doesn't work, claiming universality becomes impossible, which makes the theory section even harder to justify. And if both the theory and 14B results are weak, we're left with just empirical findings at 1B, which maximizes the risk of the paper being too narrow.\n\nI'm also realizing that existing detectors partially working would undermine the \"new task\" framing. So the tiers need to represent coherent scenarios where these factors interact, not independent axes.\n\nFor the actual metrics, I'm looking at what would determine the paper's strength: within-strategy accuracy across model sizes, cross-model generalization, cross-strategy pipeline performance, the 14B results, consistency of the EDD findings, how universally the feature patterns hold, and how reviewers would weight everything. Most of these are already locked in from completed experiments—the real unknowns are the 14B results, how well I can write the theory section, whether existing baselines exist, and how reviewers balance the positive results against the limitations.\n\nI'm about to write out the YAML structure to formalize these tiers, but I need to be careful about what's already determined versus what's still uncertain. has the 3×3 results (9 cells completed), cross-model LOO, cross-strategy LOO, pipeline, prompted gen, etc. These numbers are fixed. The tiers differ in:\n1. What the remaining experiments show\n2. How reviewers interpret the existing + new results\n3. What narrative adjustments are needed\n\nSo the \"number_ranges\" should focus on:\n- 14B results (unknown)\n- Reviewer scores (unknown)\n- Some derived metrics like \"fraction of claims that hold\"\n\nLet me think about what metrics to include:\n\nFixed metrics (same across all tiers):\n- Within-strategy 4-class acc range: 69.6-85.4%\n- Cross-model LOO mean: 66.4%\n- Cross-strategy LOO mean: 59.1%\n- Pipeline acc: 70.5%\n- Prompted gen acc: 69.5%\n- EDD at 1\n\nVariable metrics (differ across tiers):\n- 14B 4-class acc\n- 14B d1v2 AUC\n- 14B EDD\n- 14B feature overlap with 1B\n- Theory section effectiveness (qualitative)\n- Existing detector ordinal baseline acc\n- Simulated reviewer meta-score\n- Paper accept probability\n\nThe olmo_seed43_001 and pythia_seed43_001 experiments should complete across all tiers, while gpt2xl_seed43 is already done at 86.32% accuracy. For the seed robustness story, I'm comparing GPT-2 XL performance across seeds—seed42 gave 84.28% while seed43 shows 86.32%, indicating a meaningful difference to track.\n\nNow for the 14B tier, I'm weighing the Qwen2.5-14B experiments that are currently running. The prior 7B QLoRA results were confounded by quantization, but these are FP16 so cleaner. The key question is whether the perplexity-based features generalize across architectures—if tail erosion is truly architecture-independent as the model collapse theory suggests, they should work, though the magnitude might differ. LoRA fine-tuning dynamics at 14B could also behave differently than at smaller scales.\n\nLooking at the cross-scale results more carefully, there's actually quite a bit of variance: Qwen 7B hit 60.1% with a U-shape pattern, LLaMA 3 8B instruct reached 70.2%, and the base variant only got 59.9%. This suggests the 14B outcomes could plausibly range from 65-80% at best down to below 50% at worst, depending on how the architecture and fine-tuning dynamics interact.\n\nSince the qwen25_14b_scale_002 experiment uses the same LoRA methodology as the 1B runs, I should expect it to be more directly comparable than those cross-scale results. That gives me better confidence in revising my range: best case around 70-82%, median somewhere in the 58-70% band, and worst case dropping to 40-58% if the signal degrades significantly. Now I need to write out the final YAML with all these considerations in mind.\n\nLooking at the reviewer scoring, I realize the project uses its own scale rather than the standard EMNLP 1-5 system. The previous Dream had best at 3.0, median at 3.0, and worst at 2.5, but with the new theory section, 14B experiments, and seed robustness work, I should be able to push those numbers higher—potentially into the 3.5-4.0 range for best case, 3.0-3.5 for median, and 2.5-3.0 for worst case. Let me now construct the YAML output with these updated expectations.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":53851,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":53851},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d267ec99-9f6d-475d-b4aa-c1300bf1d05f","uuid":"0a9b9aaa-217d-4407-9fde-b78f0b83bdac"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BoabuZTHNCr3Fnkvn5XqdF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntiers:\n  best:\n    meaning: \"14B scale validation succeeds with strong signal, theory section provides testable predictions that match data, existing detectors fail at ordinal task — all 3 claims hold at full strength, paper type accepted as task formalization + systematic empirical study\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"14B (Qwen2.5-14B FP16) 4-class acc ≥72%, feature universality confirmed (p90_ppl + var_surprisal both in top-3)\"\n      - \"14B EDD=4, consistent with 3/3 models at 1B scale → EDD cross-scale consistency claim enabled\"\n      - \"Theory section derives EDD upper bound from Dohmatob contraction rates that matches empirical EDD=4; tail erosion mechanism (Shumailov) explains p90_ppl/var_surprisal dominance with quantitative prediction\"\n      - \"Binoculars/Fast-DetectGPT ordinal calibration ≤40% 4-class acc → confirms ordinal depth is genuinely new capability\"\n      - \"3/3 model seed stability confirmed (GPT-2 XL Δ=2.0pp, Pythia Δ=0.6pp, OLMo Δ<3pp) — variance well below method gap\"\n      - \"Instruct model weakness (47.4%) accepted as expected RLHF alignment tax, brief mechanistic explanation sufficient\"\n    number_ranges:\n      14b_4class_acc: [0.72, 0.82]\n      14b_d1v2_auc: [0.80, 0.92]\n      14b_edd: [4, 4]\n      14b_top2_feature_overlap_with_1b: [2, 2]  # both p90_ppl and var_surprisal\n      existing_detector_ordinal_4class_acc: [0.25, 0.40]\n      seed_robustness_max_delta_pp: [0.6, 3.0]\n      theory_edd_bound_vs_empirical_error: [0, 1]  # predicted EDD minus observed EDD\n      simulated_reviewer_meta_score: [3.5, 4.0]\n      accept_probability: [0.55, 0.75]\n    narrative_direction: >\n      Original framing 完全成立。三条 Claims 无需调整强度。\n      Title: \"Estimating Recursive Generation Depth: A Depth Degradation Atlas for Multi-Generational Synthetic Text\"。\n      Claim 1 (\"feasible, accurate, robust across scales\"): 14B 结果直接支撑 \"1B-14B scale robustness\" 子句，prompted gen 支撑 ecological validity。\n      Claim 2 (Atlas as PRIMARY): 理论节提供 tail erosion → p90_ppl 的因果链 + EDD 上界推导，Atlas 从纯经验观察升级为 \"theory-guided empirical characterization\"。\n      Claim 3 (cross-strategy pipeline): +11.5pp 改进 + 已确认的 temperature convergence 机制 = 完整贡献。\n      Discussion: instruct model 作为 \"alignment regularizes depth signal — future work on instruction-tuned chains\"，不削弱主体。\n      定位: EMNLP \"New Missions\" track，task formalization + theory-grounded Atlas + practical framework。\n      与 prior Dream (3.0/3.0/2.5) 的差异: theory section 填补 \"no algorithmic contribution\" 空缺，14B 消除 \"small model only\" 降级风险，seed robustness 消除 \"cherry-picked split\" 质疑。\n\n  median:\n    meaning: \"14B shows depth signal but weaker than 1B, theory section provides post-hoc rationalization but not predictive bounds, existing detectors show weak ordinal capability — claims need scope hedging, paper is borderline accept\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"14B 4-class acc 55-72%, p90_ppl in top-5 but var_surprisal drops out of top-5 or rank inverts\"\n      - \"14B EDD=3 (d3v4 AUC < 0.75), lower than 1B's EDD=4 → scale-dependent EDD attenuation\"\n      - \"Theory section provides qualitative connection (tail erosion narrative) but cannot derive precise EDD bound; Dohmatob contraction analysis yields loose bound (EDD ≤ 8) that doesn't constrain\"\n      - \"Binoculars/Fast-DetectGPT achieve 40-55% ordinal acc → partial overlap with our shallow-depth discrimination, weakening 'new task' exclusivity\"\n      - \"3/3 seed stability confirmed but one model shows >3pp variance, requiring explicit variance-aware CI reporting\"\n      - \"Instruct model weakness + 14B signal degradation creates 'small base model only?' reviewer question\"\n    number_ranges:\n      14b_4class_acc: [0.55, 0.72]\n      14b_d1v2_auc: [0.65, 0.80]\n      14b_edd: [3, 3]\n      14b_top2_feature_overlap_with_1b: [1, 1]  # p90_ppl yes, var_surprisal no\n      existing_detector_ordinal_4class_acc: [0.40, 0.55]\n      seed_robustness_max_delta_pp: [2.0, 5.0]\n      theory_edd_bound_vs_empirical_error: [2, 4]  # bound too loose\n      simulated_reviewer_meta_score: [3.0, 3.5]\n      accept_probability: [0.25, 0.45]\n    narrative_direction: >\n      需要调整 Claims 强度，但不降级目标 venue。\n      Claim 1: \"feasible, accurate, robust across scales\" 改为 \"feasible and accurate at 1-1.5B, with attenuated but persistent signal at 14B scale\"。\n      删除 \"robust across scales\" 的无条件断言，改为 \"scale-dependent signal strength with consistent feature family (perplexity tail statistics)\"。\n      Claim 2 (Atlas): EDD 从 \"=4 across models and scales\" 改为 \"=4 at 1B, =3 at 14B, suggesting scale-dependent discriminability horizon\"。\n      理论节降格为 \"Theoretical Motivation\" 而非 \"Theoretical Analysis\"——提供 intuition 而非 derivation。\n      可以新增一条 finding: \"depth signal attenuates with model capacity, consistent with larger models producing more uniform recursive degradation\"。\n      Claim 3: 不变（跨策略 pipeline 独立于 scale）。\n      对 existing detector: 承认 Binoculars 在 d0v1 上接近我们的表现，但论证 ordinal depth (d1/d2/d3 区分) 仍是我们独有的贡献。\n      需要 AC champion 或 revision opportunity。补充实验可能包括: 14B 多策略覆盖、intermediate scale (3B/7B) 连续曲线。\n      关键 reviewer rebuttal: \"signal attenuation at scale is itself a scientifically valuable finding — it characterizes the boundary of feasibility\"。\n\n  worst:\n    meaning: \"14B produces contradictory or near-chance results, theory section cannot explain scale failure, existing detectors partially match our performance — core assumption of scale-robust depth signal fails, must reframe contribution around small-model characterization\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"14B 4-class acc <55% 或 feature pattern 与 1B 矛盾 (top features 无 overlap, 或 AUC 呈 non-monotonic pattern 如 U-shape)\"\n      - \"14B EDD ≤2，与 1B's EDD=4 严重不一致 → 'scale robustness' claim 不可维持\"\n      - \"Theory section 无法解释为何 tail erosion 机制在 14B 失效；contraction rate analysis 与 empirical data 不一致\"\n      - \"Binoculars/Fast-DetectGPT 达到 50-65% ordinal acc → 在 shallow depth (d0/d1/d2) 与 RF 性能接近，仅在 deep depth 我们有优势\"\n      - \"Instruct (47.4%) + 14B failure + base 7-8B (60%) 构成一致 pattern: 方法仅对 ≤1.5B base 模型有效\"\n      - \"Seed stability 本身 confirmed，但 reviewer 认为在 limited scope 下 seed stability 是 minimum requirement 非 contribution\"\n    number_ranges:\n      14b_4class_acc: [0.35, 0.55]\n      14b_d1v2_auc: [0.50, 0.65]\n      14b_edd: [1, 2]\n      14b_top2_feature_overlap_with_1b: [0, 0]  # different features dominate\n      existing_detector_ordinal_4class_acc: [0.50, 0.65]\n      seed_robustness_max_delta_pp: [0.6, 3.0]  # still stable, but irrelevant\n      theory_edd_bound_vs_empirical_error: [5, 999]  # theory doesn't hold\n      simulated_reviewer_meta_score: [2.5, 3.0]\n      accept_probability: [0.05, 0.20]\n    narrative_direction: >\n      核心 reframe: 从 \"scale-robust depth estimation framework\" 转向 \"characterizing the scale boundary of recursive depth signals\"。\n      仍冲 EMNLP Main，不降级，但 paper type 从 \"framework paper\" 变为 \"characterization + negative result paper\"。\n      新 story spine: \"We set out to build a universal depth estimator. We found that ordinal depth signal is strong and systematic at 1-1.5B (70-85% acc, EDD=4), but attenuates dramatically at 14B.\n      This scale-dependent collapse of the depth signal is itself a finding: it reveals that larger models produce recursively-generated text that is statistically indistinguishable from\n      earlier-generation text after fewer iterations, narrowing the window of estimability.\"\n      Claim 1: 改为 \"depth estimation is feasible for models ≤1.5B with consistent feature signatures; signal attenuates at larger scales, defining a scale boundary\"。\n      Claim 2 (Atlas): 仍为 PRIMARY。Atlas 在 1B 的 9-cell 覆盖仍然是最完整的 characterization。新增 \"scale-dependent Atlas transition\" 分析。\n      EDD 概念 reframe 为 dual: \"